5. It helps with memory and retrieval
Hyperbolic embeddings are especially attractive for memory systems because conceptual taxonomies are hierarchical.
A research-agent memory could use:
root concept near center\text{root concept near center}root concept near center
specific evidence near boundary\text{specific evidence near boundary}specific evidence near boundary
Then:
broad abstractions live near the origin;
detailed facts live farther out;
sibling concepts separate angularly;
depth corresponds to specificity.
So the geometry can encode both:
specificity\text{specificity}specificity
and
semantic branch\text{semantic branch}semantic branch
This is a better match than ordinary Euclidean embedding when your knowledge base is tree-like.
6. What happens to the “upper limit on geometric data”?
Making the space hyperbolic does not reduce the amount of possible geometric data. It changes the organizing principle.
Instead of:
flat metric+local singularities\text{flat metric} + \text{local singularities}flat metric+local singularities
you get:
negative curvature+boundary dynamics+branching geometry\text{negative curvature} + \text{boundary dynamics} + \text{branching geometry}negative curvature+boundary dynamics+branching geometry
